#### 3. CQRS (Command Query Responsibility Segregation)

**Purpose**: Separate write operations (commands) from read operations (queries). Optimize each for their specific workloads.

**Core Concept**: Traditional architectures use same data model for reads and writes. CQRS uses separate models: write model optimized for consistency and validation, read model optimized for query performance.

**Architecture**:

```mermaid
graph TB
    subgraph "Write Side"
        Command[Command:<br/>Update Member]
        CommandHandler[Command Handler]
        WriteModel[(Write Model<br/>Normalized Database)]
        EventPublisher[Event Publisher]
    end

    subgraph "Event Bus"
        Events[Domain Events]
    end

    subgraph "Read Side"
        EventSubscriber[Event Subscriber]
        Projection[Projection Engine]
        ReadModel1[(Read Model 1<br/>Member Portal View)]
        ReadModel2[(Read Model 2<br/>Reporting View)]
        ReadModel3[(Read Model 3<br/>Search Index)]
    end

    Command --> CommandHandler
    CommandHandler --> WriteModel
    CommandHandler --> EventPublisher
    EventPublisher --> Events
    Events --> EventSubscriber
    EventSubscriber --> Projection
    Projection --> ReadModel1
    Projection --> ReadModel2
    Projection --> ReadModel3

    Query[Query:<br/>Get Member Profile] --> ReadModel1

    style WriteModel fill:#ff9999
    style ReadModel1 fill:#99ff99
    style ReadModel2 fill:#99ff99
    style ReadModel3 fill:#99ff99
```

**Write Model** (Member Service):

```csharp
// Command: represents user intent to change state
public class UpdateMemberAddressCommand {
    public Guid MemberId { get; set; }
    public Address NewAddress { get; set; }
    public string Reason { get; set; }
}

// Command Handler: validates and executes command
public class UpdateMemberAddressCommandHandler {
    private readonly IMemberRepository _repository;
    private readonly IEventPublisher _eventPublisher;

    public async Task HandleAsync(UpdateMemberAddressCommand command) {
        // Load aggregate from database (write model)
        var member = await _repository.GetByIdAsync(command.MemberId);

        // Business logic validation
        if (!member.IsActive) {
            throw new BusinessRuleException("Cannot update terminated member");
        }

        // Execute command (mutates write model)
        member.UpdateAddress(command.NewAddress, command.Reason);

        // Persist to database
        await _repository.SaveAsync(member);

        // Publish domain event
        await _eventPublisher.PublishAsync(new MemberAddressUpdatedEvent {
            MemberId = member.Id,
            NewAddress = command.NewAddress,
            PreviousAddress = member.PreviousAddress,
            UpdatedAt = DateTime.UtcNow
        });
    }
}
```

**Read Model** (Member Portal):

```javascript
// Event Subscriber: listens for domain events, updates read model
eventBus.subscribe('member.address.updated', async (event) => {
    // Update read model (denormalized for query performance)
    await memberPortalReadModel.update({
        memberId: event.memberId
    }, {
        $set: {
            'profile.address': event.newAddress,
            'profile.lastUpdated': event.updatedAt
        }
    });

    // Update search index
    await searchIndex.updateDocument({
        id: event.memberId,
        address: `${event.newAddress.street}, ${event.newAddress.city}, ${event.newAddress.state} ${event.newAddress.zip}`
    });
});

// Query: retrieves from read model
async function getMemberProfile(memberId) {
    // Read from optimized read model (no joins, denormalized)
    return await memberPortalReadModel.findOne({ memberId });
}
```

**Multiple Read Models** (Different Optimization Strategies):

| Read Model | Purpose | Technology | Optimization |
|------------|---------|------------|--------------|
| **Member Portal View** | Display member profile in web portal | MongoDB | Denormalized; single document contains all data for profile page; no joins |
| **Reporting View** | Generate enrollment reports for state dashboards | ClickHouse (OLAP) | Columnar storage; pre-aggregated metrics; optimized for analytical queries |
| **Search Index** | Full-text search of members by name, address, SSN | Elasticsearch | Inverted index; fuzzy matching; autocomplete |
| **Eligibility Cache** | Real-time eligibility checks for claims processing | Redis | In-memory; <10ms latency; TTL eviction |

**Benefits**:
- **Performance**: Read models optimized for specific queries; no complex joins or aggregations at query time
- **Scalability**: Scale reads and writes independently; 100 read replicas for reporting, 5 write instances for updates
- **Flexibility**: Multiple read models from same write model; different views for different use cases
- **Fault Isolation**: Read model failure doesn't impact writes; system continues accepting updates

**Drawbacks**:
- **Eventual Consistency**: Read models lag behind write model (event propagation delay); users may see stale data
- **Operational Complexity**: Maintain multiple data stores; must rebuild read models if corrupted
- **Data Duplication**: Same data stored in multiple places (write model + multiple read models); storage costs increase

**When to Use**:
- **Read-Heavy Workloads**: 1000:1 read:write ratio; optimize reads without impacting write performance
- **Complex Queries**: Queries require joins across multiple tables or aggregations; denormalized read models eliminate joins
- **Multiple Views**: Different users need different views of same data (member portal vs. reporting dashboard)

